# Lesson 22: Sentence Rearrangement (PQRS / Jumbled Sentences)

## What This Tests
Sentence Rearrangement (also called Para Jumbles, or PQRS-type
questions) gives you a set of sentences or sentence-fragments in
random order and asks you to arrange them into a single logical,
grammatically correct sentence or paragraph. It is a **reasoning**
skill as much as a language one: you are not judging individual
grammar so much as tracing the logical thread that connects one idea
to the next.

---

## Fundamental Rules for Solving Jumbled Sentences

1. **Opening Sentence Identification:**
   * A standard starting sentence usually contains a **Subject (Noun/Pronoun)**.
   * A sentence will **NEVER** begin with $V_2$, $V_3$, Objective Case (`me`, `him`, `them`), or connecting conjunctions like `And`, `But`, `So` unless specified.

2. **Logical Flow & Subject-Verb Association:**
   * Match subjects directly with their corresponding verbs.
   * Look for mandatory pairs (e.g., `Not only ... but also`, `Either ... or`, `Hardly ... when`).

3. **Pronoun-Antecedent Rule:**
   * A pronoun (`he`, `she`, `it`, `they`, `this`, `that`) must refer
     back to a noun that has **already appeared** in an earlier part.
     A sentence/part that opens with a pronoun with no prior noun to
     refer to is very unlikely to be the opening part.
   * *Example:* If part X mentions "a young scientist" and part Y
     later says "**He** discovered...", Y must come after X.

4. **Chronological / Cause-Effect Order:**
   * Ideas usually flow in the order events actually happened, or in
     cause → effect order. Look for time markers (`first`, `then`,
     `later`, `finally`, `after that`) and logical connectors
     (`because`, `as a result`, `therefore`, `consequently`) — these
     are strong positional clues, almost never opening words.

5. **Transition / Contrast Words Signal a Middle or Closing Part:**
   * Words like `however`, `but`, `on the other hand`, `nevertheless`,
     `moreover`, `furthermore`, `in addition`, `therefore`, `thus`
     connect to something stated earlier — a part beginning with one
     of these is almost never the first part of the sequence.

6. **Definite Article ('The') vs Indefinite Article ('A/An') Clue:**
   * A noun is often first introduced with `a/an` (indefinite) and
     referred to again later with `the` (definite), once it is
     already known to the reader.
   * *Example:* "**A** boy was standing near the gate" (introduces the
     boy) comes before "**The** boy then walked inside" (refers back
     to the same boy).

7. **General-to-Specific (or Specific-to-General) Flow:**
   * Passages often move from a general statement/introduction toward
     specific details, examples, or conclusions — rarely the reverse
     within a short passage.

---

## Solved Examples from Class Notes

### Example 1:
* **Parts:**
  * P: movie tonight
  * Q: do you
  * R: to watch
  * S: the
  * O: want
* **Sequence:** **Q - O - R - S - P**
* **Sentence:** *Do you want to watch the movie tonight?*

---

### Example 2:
* **Parts:**
  * P: always favours
  * Q: Only the
  * R: Fortune
  * S: bold
* **Sequence:** **R - P - Q - S**
* **Sentence:** *Fortune always favours only the bold.*

---

### Example 3:
* **Parts:**
  * A: Mobile phones or smartphones are becoming popular all over the world
  * B: But at the same time, it also harms us in many ways.
  * C: It is the most widely used means of communication today.
  * D: Today, it is very affordable and available to everyone.
* **Sequence:** **A - C - D - B**

---

### Example 4:
* **Parts:**
  * A: A group of frogs was travelling through the woods
  * B: and two of them fell into a deep pit.
  * C: When the other frogs saw how deep the pit was,
  * D: they told the two frogs that they were as good as dead.
* **Sequence:** **A - B - C - D**
* **Sentence:** *A group of frogs was travelling through the woods and
  two of them fell into a deep pit. When the other frogs saw how deep
  the pit was, they told the two frogs that they were as good as dead.*
* **Clue used:** A introduces "a group of frogs" (indefinite), B
  continues the same event with "and", C uses "the other frogs"
  (definite — refers back to A), D follows C's time-clause with the
  main clause.

---

### Example 5:
* **Parts:**
  * A: However, over-reliance on it has started to affect our ability
    to remember things on our own.
  * B: The internet has made information available at our fingertips.
  * C: This convenience has undoubtedly saved a great deal of time and effort.
  * D: We now search online instead of memorising facts.
* **Sequence:** **B - C - D - A**
* **Sentence:** *The internet has made information available at our
  fingertips. This convenience has undoubtedly saved a great deal of
  time and effort. We now search online instead of memorising facts.
  However, over-reliance on it has started to affect our ability to
  remember things on our own.*
* **Clue used:** B is the general opening statement; C ("This
  convenience") refers back to B; D gives a specific consequence; A
  opens with **However**, a contrast word, so it must come last.

---

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

* **Do not** pick the part that "sounds nice" as the opener — check
  for a pronoun/definite-article dependency on another part first.
* **Do not** ignore punctuation — a part ending mid-clause (comma,
  no full stop) usually continues directly into the next part.
* **Do not** assume the numerically/alphabetically first-labelled part
  (A, or P) is automatically the first sentence — the labels are
  random, not sequential.
* **Do** re-read your final assembled sentence/paragraph once fully
  ordered — it must read naturally and make complete grammatical and
  logical sense from start to end.

---

## Practice Exercises

Arrange the following jumbled parts into a logical sequence.

**Set 1:**
* P: he decided to plant a tree
* Q: every year on his birthday
* R: because he loved nature so much
* S: Rohan was a young boy

**Answer:** S - R - P - Q
*(Rohan was a young boy because he loved nature so much, he decided to
plant a tree every year on his birthday.)*

**Set 2:**
* A: Reading books regularly improves vocabulary and comprehension.
* B: Therefore, students should be encouraged to read more.
* C: It also helps develop critical thinking skills.
* D: These benefits are especially important during school years.

**Answer:** A - C - D - B
*(A introduces the general claim, C adds a related benefit, D notes
when the benefits matter most, B closes with "Therefore" — a
conclusion word.)*
